  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "Which of the following relations on the set $A=\\{a,b,c,d\\}$ are transitive?\n\nI. ${R}_{1}=\\{(a,b),(c,d)\\}$\n\nII. ${R}_{2}=\\{(b,b),(c,c)\\}$\n\nIII. ${R}_{3}=\\{(a,b),(c,d),(d,b)\\}$\n\nIV. ${R}_{4}=∅$",
  "worked_solution": "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z:$\n\nMoreover, we recall the following:\n\n- The universal relation $R={A}^{2}$ is transitive since it includes all possible ordered pairs.\n\n- The empty relation $R=∅$ is vacuously transitive since the antecedent in the implication is always false, and therefore the implication is always true.\n\n- A relation containing no \"linked\" pairs is always transitive.\n\n- A relation containing only reflexive pairs is always transitive.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations:\n\n- ${R}_{1}$ is transitive since it contains no \"linked\" pairs.  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${R}_{2}$ is transitive since it contains only reflexive pairs.  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${R}_{3}$ is *not* transitive. Linking $(c,d)$ with $(d,b)$ requires that $(c,b)\\in {R}_{3}.$ However, this pair is *not* in ${R}_{3}.$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\n- ${R}_{4}$ is transitive since it's the empty relation. $\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and IV only.\"",
